WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.925
AIS is the tool that lets you put a name to that ship on the horizon.

2
00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:11.550
It stands for Automatic Identification System, and it broadcasts over VHF data channels.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.225
Each vessel sends her name, her MMSI number, position, course and speed.

4
00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:23.750
With a receiver, you can call a ship by name, and your plotter can work out the closest point of approach.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.675
Class A units are the ones required on many commercial vessels.

6
00:00:28.925 --> 00:00:31.925
Class B units are made for smaller boats.

7
00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:33.850
Now the limits.

8
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.075
Does every boat out there show up on AIS?

9
00:00:38.075 --> 00:00:39.825
Not even close.

10
00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:42.375
Most small boats don’t carry it.

11
00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.075
So it never replaces a lookout.

12
00:00:45.325 --> 00:00:47.650
And the one for the Rules exam.

13
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.875
Seeing a vessel on AIS doesn’t put you in sight of her.

14
00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:54.375
In sight still means your eyes.
